      en: "Tichitt's traditional houses are a prime example of Saharan vernacular architecture. They are built from locally sourced stone and mud, with walls up to 1 meter thick. The roofs are constructed from doum palm branches and packed earth. The houses are arranged in clusters around courtyards, which serve as private outdoor living spaces. Ornamentation is minimal, limited to painted geometric patterns on doorways. The houses have small, high windows to reduce heat gain. Some have underground rooms for storing water and food. These dwellings have been continuously inhabited for generations, yet many are abandoned due to migration.",
